# Inert 803 private candidate worklist

Source-only; no production routing, visibility decision, prepared-reader invocation, owner-currentness or database acceptance. `discover_candidate_worklist(fresh_actor, case_id, selections, keys)` invokes the actual trusted scalar closure producer inside the existing fresh-principal/default repeatable-read context. It accepts no caller-created closure, query or callback.

Original closure template IDs remain separate from the native-filtered candidate subset. The exact generic native definition/finance-grant/forecast-existence ORM filters remain scalar database filters. No definition, manifest, snapshot or Version payload is hydrated. All matching Finance bindings and leadership-mode PMO bindings are retained, including nullable PMO report context and independent Template/document/report cases. Multiple binding types on one Version, multiple bindings on a shared run and historical relevant Versions are not collapsed. Only DISTINCT relevant binding Version IDs are charged, not unrelated unbound history.

A new marker-owned ledger shares limits across ALL calls/cases in one entry: 100 each template/Version/Finance-binding/PMO-binding identities, 400 total, and 1000 producer phases. Each call performs six worklist phases (case fetch, document gate, finance grant lookup, candidate query, Finance query, PMO query). Query result caps are 101 for overflow detection. Scalar closure separately spends its existing counters and phase budget; payload ledgers remain separate. These are not a single global 400-node graph, full SQL/opaque-subquery work, CPU or memory guarantee. Duplicate IDs do not reset phase cost. Strict scalar validation precedes dedup; failures poison the original/current entry and cannot return a partial worklist.

Each private frozen result retains exact case/actor/keys/marker/default and raw-connection provenance. This witness is NOT authorization, a public token, a registry of authentic outputs or permission to accept arbitrary constructed dataclasses. Future consumers must directly compose the trusted producer or implement reviewed registration/membership checks. No current consumer is wired. Constructor permission placement in the prepared PMO reader remains a separate review: its additional document-case gates are narrower admission than native visibility; this inventory does not filter cross-case bindings to conceal that distinction.

Pure evidence: 12 isolated AST/fake-context/fake-query checks pass normal and optimized Python. They cover caps, shared wrappers/cases, phase exhaustion, strict bool handling, actor/connection rollover, native query-shape contracts, nullable and cross-case tuples, timezone-aware PMO timestamp admission (naive rejected), shared runs/coexisting binding types, actual producer union/witness and sticky late failure. These fakes do NOT establish native SQL joins, JSON lookup semantics, native privacy equivalence, query cost or database rollback. All native execution remains UNRUN. Existing policy/Finance/PMO/scalar implementations and frozen backups are unchanged.
